<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qadi Abd al-Jabbar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 935–<a href="/years/1025">1025</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Abd al-Jabbar ibn Ahmad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">Qadi</a> Abd al-Jabbar was a leading Mu'tazili theologian and jurist who served as chief judge at Ray under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>, appointed through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-sahib-ibn-abbad">al-Sahib ibn Abbad</a>. His vast work al-Mughni is the fullest surviving statement of Mu'tazili theology, covering God's unity and justice, human free will and prophecy. Much of it was rediscovered in Yemeni <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in the twentieth century.</p></div>
